10. FILTER STAGE 1 — RAW HARD FILTERING
============================================================

Apply the following mandatory hard filters.

A video is retained only if ALL conditions are satisfied:

    view_count >= 50

    subscriber_count >= 100

    duration >= 60 seconds

    description length >= 30 characters

These are HARD FILTERS.

A video failing any one of them is removed from the active population.

Do not convert these conditions into scores.

Make the thresholds configurable, with these values as defaults:

    min_views = 50
    min_subscribers = 100
    min_duration_seconds = 60
    min_description_chars = 30

The pipeline should retain a reason for rejection when practical, for debugging/auditability.

============================================================
11. FILTER STAGE 2 — LANGUAGE FILTERING
============================================================

Only English videos are allowed into semantic processing.

Use the existing language-detection infrastructure if one already exists.

If no implementation exists, introduce a small isolated language-detection component rather than coupling language detection into the YouTube connector.

The requirement is:

    English → KEEP
    Non-English → REMOVE

The search API's:

    relevanceLanguage = en

is only a search relevance hint.

It does NOT replace explicit language filtering.

The actual pipeline must still verify language.

The language decision should be auditable.

============================================================
12. FILTER STAGE 3 — SEMANTIC RELEVANCE
============================================================

Use:

    all-MiniLM-L6-v2

as the semantic embedding model.

Do NOT leave the model unspecified anymore.

The model must compare:

    concept meaning

against:

    video title + video description

For each candidate reaching this stage:

1. Create the concept embedding.
2. Create the video text representation using:
       title + description
3. Generate the video embedding.
4. Calculate semantic similarity using cosine similarity.
5. Store the resulting score as:

       semantic_score

The score must be retained per video.

Do not only retain PASS/FAIL.

============================================================
13. SEMANTIC THRESHOLD
============================================================

Current threshold:

    semantic_score > 0.5

Only videos with semantic score strictly greater than 0.5 survive.

Therefore:

    score > 0.5  → KEEP
    score <= 0.5 → REMOVE

Make the threshold configurable but use:

    semantic_score_threshold = 0.5

as the default/current production value.

Do NOT use the old TDD threshold of 0.30.

============================================================
14. FINAL POPULATION
============================================================

After semantic filtering:

    remaining videos = FINAL POPULATION

There is NO fixed final population size.

Do NOT:

- select exactly 10
- force 10
- pad the population
- downsample to 10
- use 3/4/3 tier targets
- fill missing tiers to reach 10

The final population size is:

    number of videos surviving all filters

Store:

    population_size

using this actual value.

============================================================
15. CHANNEL BUCKETING
============================================================

Apply channel-size bucketing to the FINAL POPULATION.

Use subscriber-count quartiles calculated from the population itself.

Calculate:

    Q25 = 25th percentile of subscriber_count
    Q75 = 75th percentile of subscriber_count

Assign:

    SMALL:
        subscriber_count < Q25

    MEDIUM:
        Q25 <= subscriber_count < Q75

    BIG:
        subscriber_count >= Q75

This is RELATIVE population-based bucketing.

Do not introduce absolute subscriber thresholds for the three tiers.

Do not force a fixed number of videos into each tier.

Every final-population video receives:

    channel_tier = small | medium | big

============================================================
16. CHANNEL COMPOSITION
============================================================

After bucketing, calculate:

    small_channel_count
    medium_channel_count
    big_channel_count

These counts describe the composition of the actual final population.

They must not be normalized to a fixed population size.

============================================================
17. ENGAGEMENT SIGNALS
============================================================

Implement engagement aggregation for the final population.

For every final-population video calculate:

REACH RATIO:

    reach_ratio =
        (view_count / subscriber_count) / 100

INTERACTION DENSITY:

    interaction_density =
        (like_count + comment_count) / view_count

Retain the per-video metrics.

Then aggregate across the final population:

    reach_ratio_median
    interaction_density_median

Use MEDIAN, not mean, for these two daily aggregate signals.

The formulas must remain exactly as defined above.

Handle zero/invalid denominators safely.

A subscriber count of zero must never cause a division-by-zero error.

============================================================
18. SEMANTIC AGGREGATION
============================================================

Calculate:

    semantic_score_mean

over the FINAL POPULATION.

This is separate from the semantic filtering threshold.

The threshold determines whether a video survives.

The mean describes the semantic relevance of the resulting population.

